messages:
jess: Heads up — reproducible issue with the Kotlin SDK + R8 in release builds. When token refresh happens, we sometimes send the same API request twice and users get charged twice. :grimacing:

kai: Is this only happening on auth flows or everywhere?

jess: Authenticator path. Repro steps in sample app: enable minifyEnabled true, build release, trigger a 401, token refresh fires. First request succeeds but OkHttp issues a second identical request immediately after.

olivia: Can you paste the Authenticator implementation? Common pitfall is creating a new OkHttpClient inside Authenticator which can change dispatcher behavior.

jess: Simplified snippet: ```kotlin
class TokenAuthenticator(val authClient: OkHttpClient, val tokenStore: TokenStore): Authenticator {
  override fun authenticate(route: Route?, response: Response): Request? {
    synchronized(this) {
      val newToken = refreshToken() // network call using authClient
      return response.request.newBuilder()
        .header("Authorization", "Bearer $newToken")
        .build()
    }
  }
}
```

sam: Calling authClient synchronously from the Authenticator can deadlock if they share dispatcher, but duplication is odd.

jess: The twist: only happens in release with R8. Debug builds fine. Stack traces show authenticate() invoked twice; one trace has obfuscated method names.

olivia: Did R8 strip some classes used for dedupe? Any ProGuard rules included?

jess: We only kept okhttp3 classes, that didn't help. Adding `-keep class com.redwood.sdk.** { *; }` removed the duplicate. Turns out the SDK uses a tiny RequestId tag class to avoid rebroadcasting requests; R8 was stripping its metadata.

kai: So request.tag(RequestId::class.java) returned null on both attempts and dedupe failed. That fits.

saman: (typo) I mean sam: Right. tag-based dedupe depends on the tag type being present. R8 can remove it.

jess: Quick workaround: add `-keep class com.redwood.sdk.auth.RequestId { *; }` to sample app proguard rules. Longer term we should not rely on tag class preservation.

olivia: Agreed — header fallback is safer. Headers survive R8/ProGuard and interop with Java SDK is easier.

kai: Proposal: SDK v1.2.4 adds X-Redwood-Req-Id header; SDK sets it by default and uses header for dedupe if tag is missing. Also update quickstart with recommended ProGuard rules.

jess: I'll open a PR to add header fallback and update README and sample app proguard rules.

sam: Add a CI test: build release with minifyEnabled true, run the E2E flow that triggers token refresh and assert only one outgoing API call. We don't currently run minified E2E in CI.

build-bot: CI job queued: android-release-e2e-minified -> #4821

jess: Noted: observed on physical devices Android 12/13; emulator x86 didn't reproduce. Likely timing/dispatcher differences.

olivia: I'll add the rules to quickstart and a note: "If using code shrinking, include the supplied R8/ProGuard rules or upgrade to SDK v1.2.4+ which includes header-based dedupe."

kai: Sample gradle snippet for docs:
```gradle
android {
  buildTypes {
    release {
      minifyEnabled true
      proguardFiles getDefaultProguardFile('proguard-android-optimize.txt'), 'proguard-rules.pro'
    }
  }
}

// proguard-rules.pro
-keep class com.redwood.sdk.auth.RequestId { *; }
-keep class com.redwood.sdk.** { *; }
```

jess: Thanks, opening RED-2301: sample app + proguard docs + header fallback. I'll tag @olivia for docs and @sam for CI test. :thumbsup:

olivia: Will add to the SDK parity checklist so Java SDK matches behavior.

sam: Once CI green, cut a patch release so users with R8 get the header fallback ASAP.

jess: Filed: https://redwood.internal/issue/RED-2301 — closing the loop here, thanks all.
